A||||5||Terrybogin||Not overheating, but not cooling either????||||1||
Z||000000||Terrybogin||06-30-2000||11:22 PM||||My GT has been running a little warm, so I swapped in a 180 thermo. Even at highway speeds, the engine still runs at better than 200 degrees. Is this normal? When you turn on the AC, the temp will slowly come back down to about 190, but climbs back up when the air is off at normal speeds. Is this why so many people are putting hood vents in their cars?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Megafiero||06-30-2000||11:35 PM||||You may want to consider a lower thermistate, I am running a 160 thermo and fan switch on my 85 GT' and the temp never gets higher then 1/4 of the way up. I do save a little on the heat becuase I have ceramic coated headers too.   Well  good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by Megafiero (edited 06-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sillhouette.com||06-30-2000||11:50 PM||||I think it's time for a new radiator.  Or at least flush the thing good.  My 87 GT hovers at the 1/4 mark without any mods it's stock. When I'm stuck in a traffic jam the needle will go just above half way mark and than the fan kicks in and the temperature moves back down to the 1/4 mark.  <BR>My reason for the hood vents was mostly to reduce the pressure from under the hood to keep the famous Fiero push down and the headlight doors down as well.  I guess cooler radiator was a bonus.<P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-01-2000||06:15 AM||||You didn't mention if your fan was functioning properly or not, or if you've made any cosmetic changes to the facia. It sounds as though your air flow is insufficient to the radiator. When you turn the a/c on the fan comes on automatically, which would explain why your temp drops. That suggests that normal air flow is being interfered with (or that your fan isn't working except when the a/c is on). Vents might help, but you shouldn't need them. Might just need to take it to the car wash, and clean the radiator from the back. Lift the hood, and blow water through the radiator from the inside out, that may help if you haven't made any mods to the front end that are causing the reduced air flow. Got some driving lights mounted in the facia or anything?<P>-Taiji<BR>modified 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||07-02-2000||11:18 AM||||what's happening is pretty normal with the fan off. 200 is pretty normal. (Don't forget that AC condensors add significant air restriction to the cooling system air flow.)<P>yes, part of the reason for hood venting is this problem. That and the stock fan switch doesn't activate untill 235F. You can use a low temp fan switch with no other changes.<P>don't put in a 160 thermostat untill you know what problems it can cause and how to deal with them. It's rarely just as simple as stuffing in the 160 stat.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Terrybogin||07-02-2000||11:24 AM||||Thanks for the replies. I have been using an ECM scanner to get the engine temps and it really shows how inaccurate the dash gauge is. On the temp gauge, the difference between 180 and 200 is one of the small lines on the face of the gauge. After driving all day yesterday, it never got above 210, so I can live with that.||||reg||1
